<Base title="Creator stacks" description="Save a named composition of reviewed App Composer mini-apps, keep it as a draft, preview it and publish one shareable page. Ownership, drafts and revisions stay explicit.">
<h1>Save the stack, not just the settings.</h1>
<p class="lede">A stack is a named, saved composition of reviewed mini-apps. Add an app, give it a label and a caption, reorder it, save it as a draft, preview it and publish one shareable page. The apps keep their own records, media and source.</p>
<div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/_emdash/admin/plugins/composer-stacks/stacks"><Settings2 size={18} aria-hidden="true"/>Open creator stacks</a><a class="button secondary" href="/apps">Browse the app library</a></div>
<p class="small">The workspace is behind the same invited administrator sign-in as mini-app management. There is no public signup, and a visitor cannot reach a draft.</p>
<section class="section split">
 <div>
  <h2>The whole loop.</h2>
  <ol>
   <li><strong>Discover.</strong> The library shows each app's purpose, real screen and source.</li>
   <li><strong>Add and configure.</strong> A stack holds up to twelve instances with their own labels, captions and order.</li>
   <li><strong>Save and reopen.</strong> Drafts are revision-guarded: a save from a stale tab is refused instead of overwriting newer work.</li>
   <li><strong>Preview.</strong> The preview renders the draft in the admin. It never publishes and never leaks a private record.</li>
   <li><strong>Publish and share.</strong> Publishing stores a revision and gives the stack a public address. Later edits stay private until you publish again.</li>
   <li><strong>Return and edit.</strong> Reopen the stack, change it, unpublish, duplicate or archive it.</li>
  </ol>
 </div>
 <div>
  <h2>What the first slice does.</h2>
  <ul>
   <li>One personal workspace per invited creator, with owner, editor and viewer capabilities checked on every request.</li>
   <li>Two or more named stacks with independent drafts, immutable published revisions and share addresses.</li>
   <li>Instances configured on their own from the settings each app declares, or reusing an existing instance so one set of records stays authoritative.</li>
   <li>Publish, unpublish, archive, restore, duplicate, roll a publication back, export and delete with distinct semantics.</li>
   <li>An export that contains configuration only — no app records, media or credentials.</li>
   <li>A share card and a QR code drawn from the published revision you are looking at.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="small">Still open, and labelled as such: inviting other creators, custom domains for a stack, scheduled publishing and private creator analytics. The <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/issues/112">creator dashboard epic</a> owns those.</p>
 </div>
</section>
<section class="section">
 <h2>See it working.</h2>
 <p>The two demonstration stacks make the whole loop inspectable. <strong>Fictional Trail Notes</strong> shows the same app twice — one instance configured on its own and one that reuses it — beside an independently configured Linkarooie instance. <strong>Fictional Launch Board</strong> shows three apps that declare no settings at all. Both are fiction, labelled in their own names, in their own workspace.</p>
 <pre><code>node scripts/seed-demo-stacks.mjs
npx wrangler d1 execute app-composer-emdash --local --file output/demo-stacks.sql</code></pre>
 <p><a href="/demo">See the connected pair</a> · <a href="/release">Read what is verified</a> · <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/issues/114">Follow the stack work</a><ArrowRight size={16} aria-hidden="true"/></p>
</section>
</Base>
